A||N||3||FieroJoe||Pulling codes left and right!||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||04-24-2001||10:13 PM||||Well of course, now that I have everything back together and start driving the car, It keeps giving me codes from the computer.  Im getting really annoyed at that stupid check engine light being on!<P>So here's what I have currently:<P>Code 44 - Lean Exhaust.  Im thinking its either my 02 sensor or exhaust manifold gasket.  I have a new 02 sensor that I havent put in yet.<P>Code 15 (everyday!!!)  -  everytime I drive, it gives me this code.  I know it is this one and not 44 because the rad. fan runs while the check engine light is on.<P>Code 15:  Coolant temperature Sensor Circuit<BR>(low temperature indicated)<P>It says in my manual that code 15 will set if either of the wires connected to the sensor are open, or if coolant temperature is below -22*F for 60 seconds (and I know it isnt that!)  But, my only problem is that I dont know which sensor it is on my 87 DIS 2.5  There is a sensor on the corner of the head, and then there is one on the thermostat housing.  The electrical connector on the sensor on the corner of the head is a little bit cracked, but it dosent look like it would be shorting out.<P>So, first off, which sensor is it?  Which one is for the ECM, and which for the temp gauge?  And, if it happens to be that that connector needs to be replaced, where can I find a new pigtail to solder in?<P>Thanks all!<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wolfhound||04-24-2001||10:22 PM||||Joe, It,s the one in the base of the thermostat housing. You probably have a high idle also if it,s reading high voltage.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||04-25-2001||10:08 AM||||Well, yes I do have a high idle, at first start up, but it usually drops down to 950-1000 rpm and stays there.  Can I test it just by heating it up with my torch and checking the voltage across it?<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||04-25-2001||09:41 PM||||Well, I think I may have found the problem.  I took off the connector today to the sensor on the t-stat next, and noticed that one of the tabs on the connector (pigtail connector, not sensor connector) was bent up a little.  I bet it isnt making very good contact, so then the ECM gets all fiesty about it.  Well, I tried my best to bend it down a little bit so it would press against the tab inside the sensor.  I will see tomorrow if it gives me a code 15 again!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||3
